## 3. Legacy X-Ray `bump` / `bump#` $\rightarrow$ Energy-Conserving RTX PBR Conversion

In S.T.A.L.K.E.R. X-Ray / OGSR (`sload.h`), surface materials store packed normal/gloss data across two DXT5 textures:
- `*_bump.dds`: `R = Specular Gloss`, `G = Normal.z`, `B = Normal.y`, `A = Normal.x`
- `*_bump#.dds`: `R/G/B = Steep Parallax / Normal Error`, `A = Height / Displacement`

When feeding OGSR G-Buffer targets into **Ray-Traced Reflections** or **DLSS Ray Reconstruction**, convert legacy Blinn-Phong gloss $g \in [0, 1]$ into physically based perceptual roughness $\alpha_p$ and GGX linear roughness $\alpha$:
$$\alpha_p = \text{clamp}(1.0 - g \cdot 0.92, \, 0.04, \, 1.0), \qquad \alpha = \alpha_p^2$$
and clamp dielectric Fresnel $F_0 = 0.04$ (unless material ID indicates bare metal, where $F_0 = \text{Albedo}_{\text{linear}}$) so wet asphalt and metallic weapon receivers obey conservation of energy under ray-traced lighting.

## Windows 10/11 & Cross-Platform Parity (`windows-linux-cross-os-runtime-bridge`)
- On Windows 10/11, `D3D11_RESOURCE_MISC_SHARED_NTHANDLE` shares memory directly between D3D11 and D3D12.
- On Linux under Wine/Proton (`DXVK` + `VKD3D-Proton`), both D3D11 and D3D12 translate to `VkImage` handles backed by `VK_KHR_external_memory_win32` / `VK_KHR_ray_query` on the RTX 5060.

